</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Prompt issued to the LLM (left) and its structured JSON response (right).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Profiling Component</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This component (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Agentic Model Optimization ‣ ProfilingAgent: Profiling-Guided Agentic Reasoning for Adaptive Model Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>A) includes four agents responsible for model setup and profiling. The <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acquisition Agent</span> initiates the pipeline by retrieving a pretrained model and its processor from Hugging Face<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. It (1) configures the image processor to match the model’s preprocessing needs and (2) moves both model and processor to the target device (CPU or GPU), ensuring compatibility for downstream agents.
The <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input Shape Resolver Agent</span> automates identifying input tensor dimension for vision models, a prerequisite for accurate profiling that otherwise requires manual inspection. Classified as an <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-Interactive Agent</span>, it uses GPT-4o to generate valid input configurations from minimal context. Based on architectural metadata, the agent constructs a prompt, submits it to the LLM, and receives input shape fields (e.g., height, width, channels) in structured JSON format (Figure. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Agentic Model Optimization ‣ ProfilingAgent: Profiling-Guided Agentic Reasoning for Adaptive Model Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The result is stored for downstream use, enabling model-compatible profiling without human intervention.
The <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ProfilingAgent</span> characterizes model complexity through both static and dynamic profiling. For theoretical estimates, it uses ptflops <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> to compute <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multiply-accumulate operations (MACs)</span>, an approximation of the total floating-point operations required for a single forward pass, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parameter counts</span>, the total number of trainable weights in the model. For empirical metrics, it invokes the PyTorch Profiler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>on both CPU and GPU to capture operation-level latency, memory usage, tensor shapes, and call stacks during inference. The agent also performs manual per-layer profiling across submodules. All results are serialized and stored for downstream use by the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis Agent</span>, enabling reproducible comparisons across models or compression states. The <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Analysis Agent</span> interprets the profiling report generated by the previous stage and synthesizes a model-specific optimization plan.
This agent, categorized as an <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-Interactive Agent</span>, acts as the core of the system by querying a LLM to propose optimization strategies based on both architectural and runtime characteristics.
Given this report, the agent constructs a detailed prompt (see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Profiling Component ‣ 4 Agentic Model Optimization ‣ ProfilingAgent: Profiling-Guided Agentic Reasoning for Adaptive Model Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and instructs the LLM to return optimization suggestions in a structured JSON format.
Pruning suggestions specify candidate layers, pruning types (structured or head pruning), pruning ratios, and brief justifications.
Quantization suggestions are restricted to dynamic quantization methods, specifying the target layers, data types (either <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qint8</span> or <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">float16</span>), and accompanying rationales.
These outputs form the foundation for applying downstream compression techniques.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
