<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Pruning and Quantization Evaluation on ImageNet-1K</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluate the effect of our pruning and quantization strategies on the ImageNet-1K dataset across multiple architectures, including ViT-B/16, ResNet-101, Swin-Base, and DeiT-B/16. As summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.5 Pruning and Quantization Evaluation on ImageNet-1K ‣ 5 Results ‣ ProfilingAgent: Profiling-Guided Agentic Reasoning for Adaptive Model Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, pruning achieves moderate reductions in parameters and memory (e.g., 3.3% for ViT-B/16 and 2.9% for ResNet-101) with only minor accuracy degradation (less than 1% in most cases). In contrast, quantization yields substantially larger memory savings (approximately 74% reduction across models) and consistent inference speedups, reaching up to 1.74× on ViT-B/16 and 1.73× on DeiT-B/16, while largely preserving Top-1 accuracy. These results demonstrate the complementary strengths of pruning and quantization: pruning reduces model size with minimal accuracy loss, whereas quantization delivers significant efficiency gains in memory and latency, underscoring the effectiveness of our method for scaling ImageNet-1K models to resource-constrained environments.</p>
</div>
